---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/sections/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/ui/Section.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/ui/SectionHeading.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import Icon from '../components/ui/Icon.astro';
import { BRAND } from '../config/brand';
import { SITE } from '../config/site.js';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="About"
    title="Why this exists"
    lede="Two categories of tool, one gap between them, and a bill that grows for reasons nobody can explain."
  />

  <Section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      <p>
        Data tooling has split into two halves that do not meet. Pipeline tools are good at
        getting data from one place to another and stop the moment it lands. Analytics tools are
        good at answering questions and assume the data is already sitting somewhere convenient.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gap between them is where most people actually work — and it is filled with
        spreadsheets, one-off scripts, and a ticket queue in front of whoever can write the join.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the same time, the default answer to both halves became "send it to our cloud." That
        made sense when a laptop was weak. A current machine has more cores and faster storage
        than the servers that ran serious analytics a decade ago, and it is already paid for.
        Renting compute to process your own data, and being billed per row for the privilege,
        is now a choice rather than a necessity.
      </p>
      <p class="font-medium text-ink">
        {BRAND.name} is one engine that covers the whole path — ingest, transform, analyse — and
        runs it on hardware you already own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="subtle">
    <SectionHeading
      eyebrow="Commitments"
      title="What this will not do"
      lede="Easier to hold to when it is written down in public."
    />
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        [
          {
            icon: 'wifi-off',
            t: 'Never phone home',
            d: 'No telemetry, no account requirement, no usage metering. Not as a setting you can switch off — as an absence of the code that would do it.',
          },
          {
            icon: 'file-code-2',
            t: 'Never hide what it runs',
            d: 'Every generated query stays readable and editable. A tool that will not show you its output is a tool you cannot review, debug or leave.',
          },
          {
            icon: 'git-branch',
            t: 'Keep the core open',
            d: 'The engine is open source. Paid tiers add team features; they do not hold the engine hostage.',
          },
          {
            icon: 'infinity',
            t: 'Never bill per row or per seat',
            d: 'Pricing that grows with your data punishes you for succeeding. Pricing per seat decides who in your company is allowed to ask a question.',
          },
        ].map((c) => (
          <div class="flex gap-4 rounded-card border border-line bg-raised p-6">
            <span class="flex size-10 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-primary-wash">
              <Icon name={c.icon} size={19} class="text-primary" />
            </span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink">{c.t}</h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{c.d}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Status" title="Where this actually is" />
      <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <p>
          {BRAND.name} is pre-launch. The engine is in development and nothing has shipped. This
          site describes what is being built, and it is deliberately free of the things
          pre-launch sites usually reach for: there are no benchmark figures, no customer logos,
          no testimonials and no compliance badges, because none of those exist yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a marketing decision as much as an ethical one. Anyone evaluating a data tool
          has been burned by a landing page whose numbers evaporated on contact. Being the site
          that does not do that is worth more than an invented throughput figure.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first release will appear on the repository. Watching it there costs you nothing
          and tells us nothing about you.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <Button href={SITE.github} external>
          <Icon name="simple:github" size={17} />
          Watch the repository
        </Button>
        <Button href="/contact" variant="secondary">Get in touch</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
